From: Robin Snyders <robin@snyders.xyz>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] misc: fastrpc: allocate sensors messages from the reserved pool

The sensors protection domain does not reach the message buffer through its
SMMU context bank. Given a mapped buffer it addressed memory that was not
there: first faulting cleanly on the session identifier folded into the upper
bits, then, once that was corrected, stalling the interconnect for several
seconds until the watchdog reset the SoC. Neither run produced a translation
fault at the corrected address, which is what a context-bank access would
have produced.

Allocate its messages from the reserved pool instead, the same way domains
without a session already do.

Signed-off-by: Robin Snyders <robin@snyders.xyz>
---
diff --git a/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c b/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c
index 3789235..31527f5 100644
--- a/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c
@@ -954,7 +954,13 @@ static int fastrpc_get_args(u32 kernel, struct fastrpc_invoke_ctx *ctx)
 
 	ctx->msg_sz = pkt_size;
 
-	if (ctx->fl->sctx->sid)
+	/*
+	 * The sensors domain reaches the message buffer directly rather than
+	 * through its context bank, so it needs one from the reserved pool.
+	 * A context-bank mapping leaves it addressing memory that is not
+	 * there, which stalls the interconnect rather than faulting.
+	 */
+	if (ctx->fl->sctx->sid && ctx->fl->pd != SENSORS_PD)
 		err = fastrpc_buf_alloc(ctx->fl, dev, pkt_size, &ctx->buf);
 	else
 		err = fastrpc_remote_heap_alloc(ctx->fl, dev, pkt_size, &ctx->buf);
@@ -1126,6 +1132,9 @@ static int fastrpc_invoke_send(struct fastrpc_session_ctx *sctx,
 	msg->sc = ctx->sc;
 	msg->addr = ctx->buf ? ctx->buf->phys : 0;
 	msg->size = roundup(ctx->msg_sz, PAGE_SIZE);
+
+	dev_info(sctx->dev, "invoke handle %u pd %d sid %d addr 0x%llx\n",
+		 handle, fl->pd, sctx->sid, msg->addr);
 	fastrpc_context_get(ctx);
 
 	ret = rpmsg_send(cctx->rpdev->ept, (void *)msg, sizeof(*msg));
